Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have recently provided a versatile material platform to study artificial photosynthesis and photocatalysis,1-3 including proton reduction, $^{4-7}\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction,8-12 water oxidation,13 and organic photocatalysis.14-17 Structural regularity and synthetic tunability of MOFs allow hierarchical integration of multiple functional moieties, including photosensitizers (PSs) and catalytic centers, to facilitate multi-electron or radical transfer in photoreactions.2, 18 However, due to intrinsically high absorptivity of most PSs, only superficial layers of MOFs are involved in photoreactions. Light scattering by MOF particles also reduces photon utilization. Furthermore, photocatalytic efficiency of MOFs is limited by restricted diffusion of radicals and other energetic intermediates through MOF channels. By reducing one dimension of MOFs to a single layer, metal-organic layers (MOLs) have recently emerged as a new class of functionalizable 2D materials for many applications.19-22 We believe that MOLs can overcome the aforementioned issues for MOFs due to their thinness and hold great promise for applications in artificial photosynthesis and photocatalysis.
$\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru MOLs were synthesized through a solvothermal reaction between $\mathrm{HfCl}_{4}$ and bis $^{(2,2^{\circ}}$ -bipyridine) $[5,5^{\prime}$ -di(4-carboxylphenyl)- $^{\cdot2,2^{\cdot}}$ -bipyridine]ruthenium $(\mathrm{II})$ dichloride $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-R}\mathrm{u})$ , in N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with trifluoroacetic acid (TFA) and water as modulators (Figure S1, SI). $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru}}$ is proposed as the first MOL constructed from $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ SBUs and laterally connected linear L-Ru linkers to form an infinite 2D network of kagome dual $(\mathbf{kg}\mathbf{d})$ topology. Each $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ SBU is also vertically capped by six TFA groups. $\operatorname{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru thus has a formula of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}(\upmu_{3}-$ $\mathrm{O)_{8}(\upmu_{3}\mathrm{-OH)_{8}(\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-OH)_{6}(T F A)_{6}(L\mathrm{-Ru)_{6}}}}}$ . 2D $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru MOL forms in part due to stronger Hf-carboxylate bonds in the lateral direction than those in the vertical direction as we recently observed in $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -based MOFs.39
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) imaging showed $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\mathrm{-Ru}$ as flat nanoplates of ${\sim}100~\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter (Figure 1a). The low contrast of the TEM images is consistent with the monolayer nature of $\operatorname{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru, which is confirmed by a nanoplate thickness of ${\sim}1.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ observed by atomic force microscopy (AFM, Figure 1d, e and Figure S4, SI). This thickness is consistent with the modeled height of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ clusters capped with TFA groups $(\sim1.7$ nm, Figure 1f). High-resolution TEM (HRTEM) imaging confirmed the topological structure of ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru}}$ with lattice points corresponding to $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ SBUs and fast Fourier transform (FFT) revealing a six-fold symmetry (Figure 1b) that is consistent with the projection of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru}}$ structure along the vertical direction. The distance between adjacent lattice points in HRTEM images was measured to be $2.7~\mathrm{nm}$ , matching the distance between adjacent $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ SBUs. Moreover, the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern of ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru}}$ matched well with that simulated from the proposed $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ MOL structure (Figure 1c).40 All of these data support the proposed monolayer structure of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru}}$ . Furthermore, the $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectrum of digested $\operatorname{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru showed all signals corresponding to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{R}\mathrm{u}$ without any other aromatic signals, which confirms the presence of only L-Ru ligands in $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru
$\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\mathrm{-Ru}$ was modified with 2-( $5^{\prime}$ -methyl-[2,2'-bipyridin]-5- yl)acetic acid (H-MBA) ligands on the surface to afford $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -RuMBA by replacing weakly coordinating TFA groups on SBUs with MBA ligands. The $\mathrm{^1\dot{H}}$ NMR spectrum of digested $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -RuMBA gave an MBA to L-Ru ratio of ${\sim}1{:}1$ (Figure 2a and S8, SI), indicating complete replacement of TFA groups in $\operatorname{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru-MBA to give a formula of $\mathrm{Hf_{12}(\upmu_{3}\mathrm{-O)_{8}(\upmu_{3}\mathrm{-OH)_{8}(\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-OH)_{6}(M B A)_{6}(L\mathrm{-}}}}}$ $\mathrm{Ru})_{6}$ . Additionally, no signal corresponding to TFA was detected in the ${}^{19}\mathrm{F}$ NMR spectrum of digested ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-MBA}}$ (Figure S9, SI). PXRD studies indicated that ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-MBA}}$ exhibited the same structure as $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru}}$ (Figure 1c).
${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-MBA}}$ was then metalated with $\operatorname{Re}(\mathrm{CO})_{5}\mathrm{Cl}$ or $\mathbf{Mn(CO)_{5}B r}$ to afford ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Re}}$ with the catalytic center $\mathrm{Re(MBA)(CO)_{3}C l}$ that is an analogue of $\mathrm{Re^{I}(b p y)(C O)_{3}C l}$ or $\operatorname{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru-Mn with the catalytic center $\mathrm{Mn(MBA)(CO)_{3}B r}$ that is an analogue of $\mathrm{Mn^{I}(b p y)(C O)_{3}B r}$ . $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Re}}$ and ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Mn}}$ showed similar sizes and morphologies to $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\mathrm{-Ru}$ by TEM (Figure 2b and Figure S15, SI) and maintained the same structure as $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru as indicated by PXRD (Figure 1c). AFM studies showed that the thickness of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Re}}$ and ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Mn}}$ increased to ${\sim}3.7~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2d, e, Figures S14, and S16, SI), which is consistent with the height of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ clusters capped with MBA ligands $(3.2–4.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ , Figure S19, SI). In ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Re}}$ and ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Mn}}$ , each catalytic center is adjacent to six L-Ru ligands, which enables multi-electron transfer from ${\bf L}{\bf-}\mathrm{Ru}$ to $\mathrm{Re(MBA)(CO)_{3}C l}$ or $\mathrm{Mn(MBA)(CO)_{3}B r}$ . Electron transfer was further facilitated by a short distance of $1.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ between each catalytic center and its nearest L-Ru ligand (Figure S20, SI).
Re and Mn coordination environments of ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Re}}$ , $\operatorname{Hf}_{12}$ -RuMn, and $\mathrm{Re(MeMBA)(CO)_{3}C l}$ and $\mathrm{Mn(MeMBA)(CO)_{3}B r}$ {MeMBA $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ methyl 2-( $5^{\prime}$ -methyl-[2,2'-bipyridin]-5-yl)acetate, Figure S10-13, SI} were determined by $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray absorption spectroscopy. $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Re}}$ and $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru-Mn showed similar extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) profiles to $\mathrm{Re(MeMBA)(CO)_{3}C l}$ and $\mathrm{Mn(MeMBA)(CO)_{3}B r}$ (Figure S22, SI), which were well fitted with their corresponding molecular models $[\mathrm{Re(bpy)(CO)_{3}C l}$ for $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Re}}$ and $\mathrm{Mn(bpy)(CO)_{3}B r}$ for $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Mn}}$ , Figure 2c, S23-26, SI]. Moreover, $\mathrm{Hf}_{12^{-}}\mathrm{Ru}{-}\mathrm{Re}$ and ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Mn}}$ showed similar CO stretching vibrations and characteristic UV-Vis absorptions when compared to ${\mathrm{Re}}({\mathrm{MeMBA}})({\mathrm{CO}})_{3}{\mathrm{Cl}}$ and $\mathrm{Mn(MeMBA)(CO)_{3}B r,}$ respectively (Figure S17-18, SI). These results indicate that $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Re}}$ and ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Mn}}$ exhibit the same Re and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ coordination environments as molecular photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction catalysts $\mathrm{Re}^{\mathrm{I}}(\mathrm{bpy)(CO)_{3}C l}$ and $\mathrm{Mn^{I}(b p y)(C O)_{3}B r}$ , respectively.
$\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Re}}$ and ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Mn}}$ provide an excellent opportunity to test whether capping the surface of photosensitizing ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru}}$ with $\mathrm{Re(MBA)(CO)_{3}C l}$ or $\mathrm{Mn(MBA)(CO)_{3}B r}$ catalyst could facilitate multi-electron transfer from photoexcited $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru to catalytic Re or Mn centers to drive efficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction (Scheme 1). The catalytic activities for visible-light driven $\lambda>400~\mathrm{nm}$ ) $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction of ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Re}}$ , ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Mn}}$ , and homogenous controls $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-Ru}$ plus $\mathrm{Re(MeMBA)(CO)_{3}C l,}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-Ru}$ plus $\mathrm{{Mn}(M e-}$ MBA) $(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Br})$ ) were studied in an oxygen-free $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ solution with saturated $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , triethanolamine (TEOA), and 1-benzyl-1,4- dihydronicotinamide (BNAH) or 1,3-dimethyl-2-phenyl-2,3- dihydro-1H-benzo[d]imidazole (BIH) as sacrificial electron donor. The amount of generated CO was quantified by gas chromatography (GC) analysis of the headspace gas. The turnover number (TON) [defined as $n(\mathrm{CO})]$ reached 3849 (BIH) or 2092 (BNAH) for $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Re}}$ and 1367 (BIH) or 240 (BNAH) for ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Mn}}$ after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ irradiation (Figure 3a, b), which are superior to previously reported molecular systems (Table S6, SI). In comparison, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{R}\mathrm{u}$ plus $\mathrm{Re(MeMBA)(CO)_{3}C l}$ and ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\bf L}{\bf-R}{\bf u}$ plus $\mathrm{Mn(MeMBA)(CO)_{3}B r}$ exhibited modest TONs of ${<}54$ under the same conditions. Greater than 70-fold increase in catalytic activity for ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Re}}$ and ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12^{-}}{\mathrm{Ru}}{-}{\mathrm{Mn}}$ over their homogenous controls confirms the important role of hierarchical organization of PSs and Re or Mn catalytic centers in the MOLs in facilitating multielectron transfer processes to drive photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.
The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction product selectivity for $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru-Re and $\operatorname{Hf}_{12}$ - Ru-Mn was examined by quantifying the amounts of HCOOH generated using high performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) (Figure S27 and Table S5, SI). Similar to previously reported molecular systems,38, 41 $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru-Re showed a ${\sim}98\%$ selectivity for CO, while ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Mn}}$ generated more HCOOH with a $82\%$ selectivity for CO.
To confirm the structural stability of the MOLs following 24- hour $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru, $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru-Re, and $\operatorname{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru-Mn were centrifuged from the reaction suspensions. The recovered MOLs showed the same PXRD patterns as freshly prepared MOLs (Figure 3c) with $<1\%$ leaching of Hf by inductively coupled plasma - mass spectrometry. The recovered MOLs also showed similar CO stretching vibrations and characteristic UV-Vis absorptions to freshly prepared MOLs (Figure S28, 29, SI). To further confirm the photocatalytic durability of these MOLs, $\operatorname{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru-Re was used in 5 consecutive cycles of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, showing consistent catalytic activity with a total TON of 8,613 (Figure 3d).
The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction mechanism was investigated by photophysical and electrochemical studies. The phosphorescence spectra of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru were measured with the addition of different equivalence of BIH or $\mathrm{Re(MeMBA)(CO)_{3}C l}$ . As shown in the Figure S30, the phosphorescence of $\operatorname{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru was efficiently quenched by BIH but not by ${\mathrm{Re}}({\mathrm{MeMBA}})({\mathrm{CO}})_{3}{\mathrm{Cl}}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ , indicating that the excited $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru was reductively quenched by BIH to generate $[\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\mathrm{-Ru}]^{-}$ (Figure S30, SI). Cyclic voltammetry (CV) and differential pulse voltammetry (DPV) studies demonstrated that $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ru}$ had similar reduction potential to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-Ru}$ $(\mathrm{E}^{\mathrm{red}}{}_{1/2}=-0.89\mathrm{~V~}$ vs SCE, corresponding to $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\mathrm{-Ru}$ to $[\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\mathrm{-Ru}]{\-\gamma}$ ), which is negative enough to reduce $\mathrm{Re(MeMBA)(CO)_{3}C l}$ $\mathrm{~E^{red}}_{1/2}=-0.34\mathrm{~V~}$ vs SCE, Figure S31 and Table S7, SI). Moreover, we showed that the photocatalytic efficacy of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Re}}$ system is sensitive to the distance between L-Ru photosensitizing center and the capping Re catalytic center (Figure S32-34 and Table S8, SI).
We next examined $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Re}}$ catalyzed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction upon sunlight irradiation. The $\mathrm{Hf}_{12^{-}}\mathrm{Ru}{-}\mathrm{Re}$ reaction suspension was prepared as above, and placed near a window inside a chemistry laboratory at the University of Chicago. This suspension was stirred from 11:00 am to $5{:}00~\mathrm{pm}$ on Jun. $18^{\mathrm{th}}$ to Jun. $24^{\mathrm{th}}$ in 2018 (Figure S35, SI). Remarkably, $\operatorname{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru-Re catalyzed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction under sunlight irradiation with unprecedented efficiency, reaching a TON of 670 in 6 h (Figure 4). The effectiveness of ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-Re}}$ catalyzed sunlight-driven $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction was understandably weather dependent, with the highest TON of 670 achieved on sunny Jun. $18^{\mathrm{th}}.$ , 2018 and the lowest TON of 138 observed on rainy Jun. $22^{\mathrm{nd}}$ , 2018. To the best of our knowledge, this work presents the first study of sunlight-driven $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction using a combination of $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ as a PS and $\mathrm{Re}^{\mathrm{I}}(\mathrm{bpy)(CO)_{3}C l}$ as a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction catalyst.
